DANOS VRRP core: packet-socket receive, interface state, address owner

The pieces here all touch the same files (netlink, vrrp, scheduler, track,
if) and cannot be separated cleanly on 2.3.3, so they are kept together:

  * Packet-socket receive. DANOS receives VRRP adverts on the base interface
    -- with RFC mode they leave via the VMAC interface but still arrive on
    the base one -- which a raw IP socket cannot express. open_vrrp_read_socket()
    reads from a PF_PACKET socket bound by interface index and filters for
    VRRP in BPF; the receive path parses the Ethernet header, reads TTL and
    source address from the IP header, and uses PACKET_AUXDATA to drop
    VLAN-tagged frames the control plane should have stripped.

  * Interface state. An interface is only usable if its carrier is up, so
    track IFLA_CARRIER and fold it into IF_FLAGS_UP(). Only the base
    interface's flags drive instance state; a VMAC's flags must not override
    them.

  * Address owner. Record the configured priority separately from the
    running one and derive addr_owner from it, so IPAO can be detected as
    addresses come and go.

  * Deliberate deviations from upstream: native_ipv6 no longer forces
    VRRPv3, and a v2/IPv6 combination is only rejected in strict mode.

